{"tool":"exec_command","command":"cat > /home/scott/Documents/agi-pnis/gov-lab/standards/principles/principles.md <<'EOF'\n# Engineering principles for justified complexity\n\n## Purpose and scope\n\nThis standard guides design, implementation, and review decisions made by human developers and coding agents.\n\nCentral rule:\n\n> Complexity is justified only by a demonstrated requirement, constraint, or existing burden.\n\nDo not introduce complexity that is unnecessary, speculative, premature, or insufficiently justified.\n\n## Scope\n\nThis standard applies to all code changes, interfaces, data models, configuration, infrastructure decisions, build/deploy changes, and operational changes in this project.\n\nThe canonical sequence in this document is fixed. It must be preserved as written.\n\n## Canonical decision sequence (fixed)\n\n- YAGNI is the first decision gate.\n- KISS is the governing principle across all later decisions.\n\n```text\nIs this needed now?                           YAGNI\nWhat is the simplest adequate solution?         KISS (governing)\nAm I introducing an abstraction?              Rule of Three\nWhat mechanism should implement it?             Principle of Least Power\nHow should responsibilities be organized?       Separation of Concerns\nHow should behavior be assembled?              Composition over Inheritance\nDo several adequate options remain?            Occam's Razor\n```\n\n## Canonical principles in operation\n\n| Step | Canonical principle and role | Decision rule | Constraint added for later steps |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | YAGNI — first scope gate | Do not add capabilities, extensions, abstraction, options, configuration, or mechanism options unless a present requirement, constraint, or demonstrated burden demands them. | If no present basis exists, work is rejected or deferred. No later principle may add the same rejected scope. |\n| 2 | KISS — governing principle | Keep the accepted scope at the lowest total affected-system complexity while still satisfying requirements and constraints. Total complexity includes concepts, states, dependencies, indirection, configuration, coordination, operational burden, testing burden, deployment work, and maintenance cost. | Every later choice must be adequate and not increase avoidable complexity. |\n| 3 | Rule of Three — abstraction gate | Treat three similar cases as a repetition heuristic, not an automatic threshold. Generalize only when repeated evidence shows a stable shared boundary and abstraction cost is justified. | If repetition is insufficient or shared shape is unclear, continue with a concrete implementation and skip abstraction at this step. |\n| 4 | Principle of Least Power — mechanism choice | Choose the least capable mechanism that can satisfy the accepted design. | Capabilities must be justified by failure of a weaker mechanism, not by convenience or fashion. |\n| 5 | Separation of Concerns — structural rule | Create boundaries only when distinct responsibilities, ownership, risks, lifecycles, or change drivers are materially separable and the boundary reduces total complexity. | Keeps or adds only meaningful boundaries; no extra split simply to satisfy naming or layering aesthetics. |\n| 6 | Composition over Inheritance — structural preference | Use composition when behavior reuse and variation are assembled explicitly and more cheaply than subtype hierarchy. Use inheritance only where a true stable substitutable contract makes it simpler. | Prevents accidental coupling growth after earlier gates. |\n| 7 | Occam's Razor — tie-breaker | When multiple adequate designs remain, choose the design with fewer independent assumptions and mechanisms. | Prevents further expansion of scope and ends the decision by selecting the simplest adequate outcome. |\n\n## Practical conflict and exception handling\n\nAn apparent conflict is resolved by the same step order. Earlier applicable principles control later ones.\n\n- If a decision fails YAGNI, the change is rejected or deferred.\n- If KISS rejects a design, later principles cannot restore it.\n- Rule-of-Three and KISS limits early abstraction: only earlier justified repetition or constraints can reopen complexity.\n- If two adequate options remain, Occam's Razor is the final tie-breaker.\n- A new requirement, constraint, or burden may reopen review only with new evidence for the current work.\n\n## Material complexity justification test\n\nBefore adding any material complexity, the proposing or coding agent MUST record:\n\n1. The present requirement, constraint, or demonstrated burden being addressed.\n2. The simplest adequate alternative considered without that complexity.\n3. Why that alternative is inadequate for current needs.\n4. Added material complexity (concepts, states, dependencies, indirection, configuration, coordination, operational, or maintenance burden).\n5. Why the addition reduces net complexity or risk for the affected system.\n6. How the decision will be validated and monitored.\n\nComplexity is justified only if this record shows a present need that the simpler alternative cannot satisfy, or a current burden that the added complexity measurably reduces.\n\n## Observable complexity criteria\n\n| Justified complexity | Unnecessary complexity |\n| --- | --- |\n| Linked to a current requirement, constraint, or demonstrated burden | Based on possible future demand, generic flexibility, or aesthetic style |\n| Removes or reduces current cost, failure risk, or maintenance burden | Adds new modes, states, config, or coordination without reducing current cost |\n| Produces a measurable reduction in total affected-system complexity | Adds abstractions or layers with no clearly reduced complexity elsewhere |\n| Can name the failure of the simpler alternative | Justified by “nice to have” or “cleaner” language alone |\n\n## Supporting concepts (outside canonical sequence)\n\nThese concepts are mandatory guidance only after the canonical sequence admits the work.\nThey cannot create new gates, reorder steps, revive rejected scope, or substitute authority.\n\n### Single Responsibility (cohesion rule)\n\n- Use to decide whether behavior belongs together inside an admitted boundary.\n- Limits: it does not require tiny classes/functions or a strict one-responsibility-per-file rule.\n- Distinct contribution: determines cohesion inside existing boundaries, not whether a new boundary is justified.\n\n### Principle of Least Astonishment (predictability rule)\n\n- Use to align behavior, defaults, errors, and naming with established expectations from callers and maintainers.\n- Limits: it does not preserve a legacy behavior that materially violates adequacy or KISS.\n- Distinct contribution: lowers cognitive and operational surprises inside already accepted work.\n\n### Law of Demeter (coupling rule)\n\n- Use to limit dependence on another component’s internals by routing requests through owning boundaries.\n- Limits: do not add wrappers or forwarding just to satisfy call depth preferences.\n- Distinct contribution: protects coupling hygiene after a boundary already exists.\n\n### Fail Fast (failure-handling rule)\n\n- Use to validate assumptions near the trust/ownership boundary and return clear failures with context when continuation is not meaningful.\n- Limits: it does not require crash-only behavior; recovery, retry, degradation, or compensation is valid when present requirements demand it.\n- Distinct contribution: defines failure-localization behavior in implementation and review.\n\n## Use by developers and coding agents\n\nFor any material change:\n\n1. State the present need and simplest adequate baseline.\n2. Apply the canonical flow in order.\n3. Keep a short justification record for any material complexity.\n4. Ensure each decision remains within the constraints already added by earlier steps.\n\nFor review:\n\n- Confirm present basis exists.\n- Confirm a simpler adequate alternative was considered.\n- Confirm no rejected step is being bypassed later.\n- Confirm supporting concepts improved the accepted design and did not add scope.\n\n## Outcome intent\n\nThis framework aims for systems that are simpler in total effect, adequate for current work, and easier to change when real requirements evolve. It prefers material simplicity, operational thrift, and explicit justification over rigid abstractions or premature architecture.\nEOF","files":["/home/scott/Documents/agi-pnis/gov-lab/standards/principles/principles.md"],"category":"read"}
